  "question": {
    "text": "A monochromatic light is incident on a metallic plate having work function $\\phi$. An electron, emitted normally to the plate from a point $A$ with maximum kinetic energy, enters a constant magnetic field, perpendicular to the initial velocity of electron. The electron passes through a curve and hits back the plate at a point $B$. The distance between A and B is :<br/>(Given : The magnitude of charge of an electron is e and mass is $\\mathrm{m}, \\mathrm{h}$ is Planck's constant and c is velocity of light. Take the magnetic field exists throughout the path of electron)"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\sqrt{\\mathrm{m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\sqrt{8 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$2 \\sqrt{\\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{KE}_{\\max }=\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi \\\\ & \\mathrm{p}=\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{mK}_{\\max }} \\\\ & \\mathrm{p}=\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} \\\\ & \\mathrm{d}_{\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{B}}=2 \\mathrm{R} \\\\ & =2\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{p}}{\\mathrm{qB}}\\right]\\end{aligned}$<br/>$\\mathrm{d}_{\\mathrm{AB}}=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)}}{\\mathrm{eB}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{8 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)}}{\\mathrm{eB}}$"
  }
